<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pope Clement VII</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1478">1478</a>–<a href="/years/1534">1534</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Giulio de' Medici · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-medici">House of Medici</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Pope Clement VII (1478–1534), born Giulio de' Medici, was pope from 1523 to 1534. The illegitimate son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/giuliano-de-medici">Giuliano de' Medici</a>, he governed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence">Florence</a> for his cousin <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-leo-x">Pope Leo X</a> before his election. His shifting alliances between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-v-holy-roman-emperor">Charles V</a> led to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sack-of-rome-1527">Sack of Rome</a> in 1527, after which he made peace with Charles and used imperial troops to restore the Medici to Florence in 1530.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pope-clement-vii.jpg" alt="Pope Clement VII" width="320" height="387" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Sebastiano del Piombo · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:El_papa_Clemente_VII,_por_Sebastiano_del_Piombo.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Giulio de' Medici was born illegitimately in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence">Florence</a> on 26 May 1478, one month after his father, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/giuliano-de-medici">Giuliano de' Medici</a>, brother of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lorenzo-de-medici">Lorenzo de' Medici</a>, was murdered in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence-cathedral">Florence Cathedral</a> in the Pazzi conspiracy. He spent his first seven years with his godfather, the architect Antonio da Sangallo the Elder, and was then raised by Lorenzo alongside the future <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-leo-x">Pope Leo X</a>. When Leo was elected in March 1513, Giulio became Archbishop of Florence within three months, and a papal dispensation recognised his birth as legitimate. Leo made him a cardinal on 23 September 1513. He governed Florence from 1519 to 1523.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>After Adrian VI's death Giulio was elected on 19 November 1523 as Clement VII, with a reputation as a statesman, though contemporaries thought him worldly and indifferent to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Reformation</a>. Charles V and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francis-i">Francis I</a> each demanded that he take a side. After Francis conquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a> in 1524 Clement joined <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> in a treaty of January 1525, but Francis was crushed and imprisoned at Pavia and Clement turned back to Charles.</p><p>His wavering led to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sack-of-rome-1527">Sack of Rome</a> in 1527, during which he was imprisoned. In 1527 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-viii-of-england">Henry VIII</a> asked him to annul his marriage to Catherine of Aragon, the emperor's aunt; Clement refused, possibly under pressure from Charles, whose effective prisoner he was. In 1533 he married his cousin's granddaughter, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catherine-de-medici">Catherine de' Medici</a>, to the future <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-ii-of-france">Henry II of France</a> at Marseille.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Clement was a patron in the Medici tradition. He commissioned or supervised <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/michelangelo">Michelangelo</a>'s Last Judgment in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sistine-chapel">Sistine Chapel</a>, the Medici Chapel and the Laurentian Library, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raphael">Raphael</a>'s Villa Madama, and he appointed Benvenuto Cellini head of the Papal Mint. In 1533 he approved Copernicus's heliocentric theory.</p><p>He died on 25 September 1534 after months of illness, having reigned ten years, and was buried in St Peter's. Contemporaries blamed poison, but his symptoms do not support that. He has been called the most unfortunate of the popes. The historian Paul Strathern writes that without his guidance the Medici could not have reached the heights they later did.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Pope Clement VII” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Medici it raised gave the Church two popes, Leo X and <b>Clement VII</b>, and later two queens of France.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-florence">Republic of Florence · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Unpaid troops of Emperor Charles V stormed Rome, killing thousands and looting churches while <b>Pope Clement VII</b> sheltered in Castel Sant'Angelo.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/may-6">6 May in history</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1532 <b>Pope Clement VII</b> brought Ancona under direct papal rule, installing a garrison and building a fortress.</blockquote><a href="/state/republic-of-ancona">Republic of Ancona</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence">Florence</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=43.77,11.25&amp;year=1514" aria-label="Florence on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4820 613 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Pope Clement VII"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5270 883h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5270 883h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=43.77,11.25&amp;year=1514">What was here in 1514? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
